ese voice actor","Seiji Kurata , Japanese photographer","Seiji Kurihara , Japanese former rugby union player","Seiji Maeda , Japanese voice actor","Seiji Maehara , Japanese politician","Seiji Mataichi , Japanese politician","Seiji Matsunaga , Japanese sport wrestler","Seiji Matsuyama , Japanese manga artist","Seiji Miyaguchi , Japanese actor","Seiji Miyane , Japanese announcer, television presenter and professional wrestling promoter","Seiji Mizushima , Japanese anime director","Seiji Morita , Japanese film editor","Seiji Naito , Japanese urologist","Seiji Nakamura , Japanese politician","Seiji Narita , Japanese former international table tennis player","Seiji Naruse , Japanese rear-admiral and engineer","Seiji Nemoto , Japanese sport wrestler","Seiji Nishimura , Japanese karateka","Seiji Nishino , Japanese neuroscientist and writer","Seiji Noma , Japanese writer and publisher","Seiji Ogawa , Japanese biologist","Seiji Oko , Japanese volleyball player","Seiji Ono , Japanese table tennis player","Seiji Osaka , Japanese politician","Seiji Ozawa , Japanese conductor","Seiji Rokkaku , Japanese actor","Seiji Saito , Japanese footballer","Seiji Sakaguchi , Japanese professional wrestler","Seiji Sasaki , Japanese voice actor","Seiji Shibata , Japanese fencer","Seiji Shimota , Japanese novelist","Seiji Shindo , Japanese football player","Seiji Shinkai , Japanese chemist","Seiji Suzuki , Japanese politician","Seiji Tahara , Japanese professional baseball pitcher","Seiji Takahashi , Japanese ice hockey player","Seiji Takaiwa , Japanese actor","Seiji Takaku, Japanese psychologist","Seiji Takeda , Japanese television producer","Seiji Tōgō , Japanese painter and artist","Seiji Tomashino , Japanese former Nippon Professional Baseball infielder/outfielder","Seiji Tsutsumi , Japanese businessperson, author and poet","Seiji Uebayashi , Japanese profesisonal baseball outfielder","Seiji Ueda , Japanese astronomer","Seiji Yamada , Japanese ski mountaineer","Seiji Yamamoto , Japanese chef","Seiji Yokoyama , Japanese composer","Seiji Yoshida , Japanese writer and communist"]}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seiji","wikidata":"Q1389324"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Spanish_jewellery-Gold_and_emerald_pendant_at_VAM-01.jpg/500px-Spanish_jewellery-Gold_and_emerald_pendant_at_VAM-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Spanish_jewellery-Gold_and_emerald_pendant_at_VAM-01.jpg/500px-Spanish_jewellery-Gold_and_emerald_pendant_at_VAM-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpanish_jewellery-Gold_and_